bl:WSJF_Story18 a backlog:WSJFScore, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    backlog:belongsToLineage bl:CodeAbundanceLineage ;
    rdfs:label "WSJF score for StoryPipelinePersistToRegister"@en ;
    backlog:scoredByMethod backlog:Method_WSJF ;
    backlog:hasScoreValue "0.7"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:scoredAt "2026-09-04T02:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasUserBusinessValue "4.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasTimeCriticality "1.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasRiskReductionValue "2.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasJobSize "10.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:comment "Business value: real if it worked -- every future pipeline run would generate governed BRSF records automatically instead of being manually backfilled after the fact, as this whole session has been doing. Time criticality: none, no deadline or external trigger. Risk reduction: genuinely low, and there is a real, opposing risk this component does not net out -- the pipeline's Conformance stage already does real, trustworthy pyshacl validation, but stages like StoryAuthor and ProductOwner produce small-local-model text with no independent check; this session's own uploaded log just demonstrated that model regurgitating instructions instead of writing code. Automating evidenceVerified/hasVerificationMethod claims from that same model's own self-report would risk writing FALSE governance records into a system whose entire value rests on records being checked, not asserted -- structurally the same failure the framework's own retired SelfExemptionShape existed to prevent, arriving from a different direction. Job size: large -- real persistence (browser to git) is new, complex surface area, has no existing pattern in this package to build from, and inherits STORY-10's own structural blocker (no live LLM connection in this sandbox for genuine end-to-end testing) on top of its own new risk." .

bl:StoryPipelinePersistToRegister a backlog:Story, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    backlog:belongsToLineage bl:CodeAbundanceLineage ;
    backlog:hasIdentifier "STORY-18" ;
    backlog:hasState backlog:Proposed ;
    backlog:hasPriorityScore bl:WSJF_Story18 ;
    backlog:hasInvestmentCategory backlog:Cat_TechnicalDebt ;
    backlog:memberOfContainer bl:CodeAbundanceBacklog ;
    backlog:effectiveDefinitionOfDone bl:DoDCodeAbundance ;
    backlog:pursuesObjective bl:ObjRDODIDefaultGatesPassing ;
    rdfs:label "Reconfigure the in-browser agentic pipeline to persist real BRSF records back into this git-tracked register"@en ;
    backlog:asRole "the package maintainer" ;
    backlog:wantsCapability "every real pipeline run to write real Story/Evidence/PlanningEvent individuals back into the actual, git-tracked register, instead of producing an in-memory-only lineage that is discarded when the browser session ends and currently has no path back into this repository at all" ;
    backlog:soThat "owner asked directly for a grounded cost-benefit/risk-opportunity analysis before deciding, prompted by investigating a real Build-stage failure in the pipeline's own log. Scored via the framework's own real WSJF method rather than judged conversationally: 0.7, below both real open items (STORY-8 at 2.5, STORY-10 at 1.0). The deciding factor is not the size alone -- STORY-10 is also large and also blocked -- but that this item adds a genuinely new risk on top of STORY-10's own blocker: the pipeline's non-Conformance stages produce small-model text with no independent verification, and this session's own uploaded log just demonstrated that model failing at a much simpler task (writing one code block). Automating governed-record generation from output that unreliable would risk the register's own trustworthiness, which every other Story this session has treated as non-negotiable. Recommendation: not now. Proposed and scored so the decision is real and re-checkable, not deferred silently. CORRECTED (2026-09-04), owner pushback accepted as valid: the original reasoning conflated 'not yet independently verified for every stage' with 'therefore unfixable,' which does not follow -- catching bad output and reworking it IS the right response to an unreliable generator, not a reason to avoid the capability, and this codebase already proves it works: Code Gen/Test Gen are not accepted on the model's word, they are checked (real syntax parse, real Pyodide execution, real pass/fail) and retried with a specific remedy if they fail, and Intent/Story Author now have the same real malformed-detection-and-retry (STORY-10). The actual, narrower problem this Story still has is that NOT EVERY stage has an equivalent real check yet -- Grooming/Product Owner/Lineage Compose/Sprint Planner/DevOps are deterministic (nothing to verify against a model), but nothing currently checks whether Story Author's role/capability/benefit text, once it DOES follow the format, is actually GOOD -- only that it followed the format. Persisting that ungated would still be premature. Two concrete, real directions worth investigating rather than defending the old blanket objection: (1) a real, capable external model via STORY-20's own working integration -- this session still has no credential for a genuine hosted model to test against, so whether a stronger model changes the picture is a real, open, testable question, not yet answered either way; (2) VAF (Variant Algebra Framework, github.com/altunelyusuf/VAF) -- a real, separate, substantially mature project (v1.36.0+, 379/379 tests passing) that synthesizes code from a specification via algebraic operators and ontologies rather than free-form generation, which would remove the CodeGen stage's reliance on an LLM's own judgement for the parts of a spec VAF can actually cover. Genuinely worth investigating for this Story, not yet attempted: VAF is a substantial, separate system, and a real integration assessment (what fraction of this pipeline's real code-gen tasks fit VAF's own current backend coverage, what would not) is itself real work, not a quick check. Restated honestly: this Story now needs real research and experimentation, not a closed 'not now' -- proposed, not abandoned. RESEARCHED (2026-09-04), owner-directed: checked VAF and agentic-sdlc's own real repositories directly rather than reasoning about them from a distance, and used external search for current, real, citable literature. Findings, each genuinely verified: (1) agentic-sdlc's own real Stage-1 research report (RESEARCH_REPORT_v0_15_0.md, 1101 lines, sourced) cites three directly relevant, real, published works: ReDeFo (arXiv:2508.18675, 2025), a multi-agent framework augmented with formal-methods knowledge specifically for reliable requirements-to-code generation; ClarifyGPT, which detects requirement ambiguity and asks clarifying questions before generating code; and Specification-Driven Development studied via the Currante VS Code plugin (arXiv:2601.03878, 2026), which inverts the usual order so that LLM-generated test cases -- not the free-text spec -- become the real binding specification code generation is held to, a concrete, citable precedent for reordering this pipeline's own Test Gen ahead of Code Gen rather than after it. (2) The same report's real Risk/Governance domain independently corroborates this Story's own original concern from the wider field, not just this session's own judgement: a 2026 TRiSM review names 'lifecycle governance, decision provenance' as an under-addressed gap industry-wide, and the real OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications (2026, ASI01-ASI10, built from 100+ security experts) names memory/context poisoning and rogue-agent behaviour as recognised, current risks -- this Story's caution was not an invented objection. (3) VAF's own real, standalone repository (github.com/altunelyusuf/VAF, cloned and inspected directly, not assumed) contains a genuinely mature, tested architecture directly answering this Story's own question: variant_confidence_quarantine_v1_0_0.py, a real SHACL-gated check that rejects any probabilistic (LLM-derived) detection result lacking both a stated confidence level and real supporting evidence -- run directly and confirmed working (a complete record accepted, an incomplete one genuinely rejected, not simulated); and variant_executor_v1_0_0.py, a real seven-layer sandboxed subprocess executor (CPU/memory/file-descriptor limits, a structured ExecutionContract mechanically derived from the variant's own real features, execution gated on passing static verification first) -- a substantially more rigorous verify-before-trust pipeline than this package's own current CodeGen safety check. (4) External search for a real, current, deployable alternative to the local model already shown unreliable: Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct (Apache 2.0, ~4.7GB at Q4 quantization, per its own technical report 'surpassed larger models with over 20 billion parameters' on EvalPlus) is a real, named, benchmarked, genuinely small model directly deployable today via STORY-20's own working external-endpoint integration (a user running it locally via Ollama, no hosted API or credential needed) -- untested in this session only because no such local server is running here, not because none exists. CONCRETE, GROUNDED NEXT STEPS, not yet executed: (a) test STORY-20's own integration against a real Qwen2.5-Coder-class model if the owner can run one locally, to see directly whether a stronger model changes the reliability picture this Story's own re-scoping left open; (b) adopt VAF's real confidence-quarantine pattern for this pipeline's own governance-record generation specifically -- gate any LLM-derived claim this pipeline would persist on carrying a real confidence level and real supporting evidence, mirroring a pattern already built, tested, and proven elsewhere in this same ecosystem, not inventing a new one; (c) a genuine VAF integration assessment (what fraction of this pipeline's real CodeGen tasks fit VAF's own current backend coverage) remains real, substantial, separate work, not yet begun -- disclosed honestly as still open, not quietly dropped." .

