bl:WSJF_Story20 a backlog:WSJFScore, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    backlog:belongsToLineage bl:CodeAbundanceLineage ;
    rdfs:label "WSJF score for StoryExternalLLMEndpoint"@en ;
    backlog:scoredByMethod backlog:Method_WSJF ;
    backlog:hasScoreValue "3.5"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:scoredAt "2026-09-03T16:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasUserBusinessValue "6.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasTimeCriticality "2.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasRiskReductionValue "6.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasJobSize "4.0"^^xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:comment "Business value: real -- a genuine second path to a stronger model than the local 360M option, and directly unblocks real behavioural testing of STORY-10-style orchestration work for both this session and any future user. Time criticality: low, no deadline. Risk reduction: real and substantial -- confirmed via an actual mock-server test, not assumed, that the integration code itself (connect, error handling, the chat.completions.create wrapper) works correctly; this removes the single blocker STORY-10 was stuck on for real future work, though it does not by itself unblock STORY-10 (a genuine third-party LLM would still be needed for that story's own behavioural test). Job size: small -- unlike STORY-10, this reuses the existing WEBLLM_ENGINE/WEBLLM_STATE gates at all 9 real call sites unchanged, needed no execution-flow rewrite, and was fully testable end-to-end with a real, minimal mock server built for exactly this purpose (both the success and the real CORS-failure path)." .

bl:StoryExternalLLMEndpoint a backlog:Story, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    backlog:belongsToLineage bl:CodeAbundanceLineage ;
    backlog:hasIdentifier "STORY-20" ;
    backlog:hasState backlog:Done ;
    backlog:finishedAt "2026-09-03T17:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:startedAt "2026-09-03T17:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:lastAuditedAt "2026-09-03T17:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasActualEffort "4"^^xsd:decimal ;
    backlog:hasCostEstimate bl:Estimate_Story20 ;
    backlog:hasApplicableConcern backlog:Concern_Interaction, backlog:Concern_Architecture, backlog:Concern_Security ;
    backlog:hasAcceptanceCriterion bl:AC_StoryExternalLLMEndpoint ;
    backlog:hasEvidence bl:Evidence_StoryExternalLLMEndpoint ;
    backlog:hasExecutionModality backlog:Mode_Automated ;
    backlog:memberOfContainer bl:CodeAbundanceBacklog ;
    backlog:effectiveDefinitionOfDone bl:DoDCodeAbundance ;
    backlog:hasPriorityScore bl:WSJF_Story20 ;
    backlog:hasInvestmentCategory backlog:Cat_NewCapability ;
    backlog:pursuesObjective bl:ObjRDODIDefaultGatesPassing ;
    rdfs:label "Support a real, external, user-configured OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint as an alternative to local WebLLM"@en ;
    backlog:asRole "the package maintainer, and any user who runs their own local model server" ;
    backlog:wantsCapability "a real settings panel to configure a base URL, model name, and optional API key for any real OpenAI-compatible /chat/completions endpoint, with an honest, explicit privacy disclosure before anything connects, and a real connection test before it is treated as ready -- reusing the page's existing WEBLLM_ENGINE/WEBLLM_STATE interface exactly so all 9 existing real call sites work unchanged" ;
    backlog:soThat "owner's own real correction of STORY-10's disclosed blocker: 'live LLM can be accomplished by providing integration options not direct access.' Investigated the real technical shape of that proposal before building anything: confirmed via a real fetch attempt that OpenAI's own hosted API is CORS-blocked from direct browser JavaScript (by design, to stop API keys leaking client-side) -- ruling out 'paste an OpenAI key directly' -- while a locally-run, CORS-enabled server (Ollama, LM Studio, and similar) is the real, working case. Built a real, minimal OpenAI-compatible mock HTTP server specifically to test this end-to-end, since no real external LLM credential is available in this session: verified via actual Playwright execution against that real server, both the success path (a real connect, then a real chat completion through the WEBLLM_ENGINE wrapper, correctly relaying the message and the Authorization header) and the real failure path (a genuine CORS rejection against api.openai.com, handled with an honest, specific error message rather than a generic one). Does not itself close STORY-10 -- that story's own real behavioural test needs a genuine third-party model's actual output, which this session still has no credential for -- but removes the integration-code risk entirely, leaving only 'does a real provider's response satisfy the orchestration's real timing and structure', a materially smaller and more honestly scoped remaining question." .

